==English==

===Etymology===
First attested in 1753 (in the theological sense); attested in 1830 in the political sense. From {{suffix|en|absolute|ism}}, based after {{der|en|fr|absolutisme}}.<ref name=SOED>{{R:SOED5|page=9}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈæb.sə.luː.tɪz.m̩/|/ˈæb.sə.ljuː.tɪz.m̩/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈæb.səˌluˌtɪz.m̩/|/ˈæb.səˌljuˌtɪz.m̩/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-absolutism.ogg|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|theology}} Doctrine of [[preordination]]; doctrine of absolute decrees; doctrine that God acts in an absolute manner. {{defdate|First attested in the mid 18<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED>{{R:SOED5|page=9}}</ref>
# {{senseid|en|politics}} {{lb|en|political science|sociology}} The principles or practice of absolute or arbitrary government; {{synonym of|en|despotism}}. {{defdate|First attested in the early 19<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED/>
#: {{hypo|en|absolute monarchy|benevolent absolutism|enlightened absolutism|enlightened despotism}}
#* {{quote-book|en|passage=The element of '''absolutism''' and prelacy was more controlling in the counsels of the rival corporation.|author=[[w:John G. Palfrey|John Gorham Palfrey]]|title=History of New England|year=1859|volume=I|chapter=IV|page=217|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=CwqUdVXqgPsC&pg=PA217&lpg=PA217}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2019| author=John Gascoigne | title=Science and the State |page=54| passage=As '''absolutism''' became more consolidated in Europe, and particularly in France, such nonabsolutist states tended to define themselves in opposition to their absolutist rivals: hence, those features of their government which differed from '''absolutism''' tended to be given special prominence. }}
# {{senseid|en|philosophy}} {{lb|en|philosophy}} Belief in a [[metaphysical]] [[absolute]]; belief in [[Absolute]]. {{defdate|First attested in the late 19<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED/>
# [[positivity|Positiveness]]; the state of being absolute.
#: {{hypo|en|Meiklejohnian absolutism}}
# {{lb|en|rare}} The characteristic of being absolute in nature or scope; [[absoluteness]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2003|author=Ruth R. Wisse|title=The Modern Jewish Canon: A Journey Through Language|isbn=0226903184|passage=It was the '''absolutism''' of his ambition to be a perfect writer (and perhaps also the perfect son) that imperiled him.}}

